#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""glaeda-mini-fleet: one declarative inventory for a fleet of cmux build Mac minis.

    glaeda-mini-fleet observe  [--manifest M] [--host H ...] [--out FILE]   probe hosts over SSH, print observed JSON
    glaeda-mini-fleet check    [--manifest M] [--host H ...] [--observed FILE] [--json]
    glaeda-mini-fleet plan     [--manifest M] [--host H ...] [--observed FILE]  print the fixes check would need
    glaeda-mini-fleet apply    [--manifest M] --host H ... [--yes]              make the additive fixes
    glaeda-mini-fleet preflight [HOST ...] [--output text|json]                 every onboarding blocker, one pass
    glaeda-mini-fleet fix       [HOST ...] [--yes]                              every repair that needs no root
    glaeda-mini-fleet sudo-plan [HOST ...] [--run [--ask-once]] [--sudoers]     every root step, one password per host
    glaeda-mini-fleet share THING --from HOST HOST ... [--yes]                  seed over the LAN from a prepared host
    glaeda-mini-fleet onboard   [HOST ...] [--yes] [--from HOST]                preflight, fix, sudo-plan, enroll
    glaeda-mini-fleet repair    [HOST ...] [--yes] [--from HOST]                preflight, fix, sudo-plan, renew, runners
    glaeda-mini-fleet upgrade   [HOST ...] --candidate-run RUN [--yes]         roll a new candidate over the fleet
    glaeda-mini-fleet pools    [HOST ...] [--observed FILE]                     runner capacity per pool label (JSON)
    glaeda-mini-fleet reserve  HOST ... --for TEXT (--hours N | --until EPOCH) [--owner O] [--force] [--yes]
    glaeda-mini-fleet borrow   [HOST ...] [--class std] --for TEXT (--hours N | --until EPOCH) [--owner O] [--yes]
    glaeda-mini-fleet release  HOST ... [--owner O] [--force] [--yes]           drop a reservation

The manifest names every host, its fleet node id (node_id, so every enrollment rerun asks for the
same one), its hardware and scheduling class, the toolchain it must carry, the SSH keys that must, may, and must
not be in the login user's authorized_keys, and the launchd jobs that must be running. `check` diffs fresh observation against it and exits 1 on drift.

observe, check, and plan are read-only. The probe (scripts/cmux_mini_probe.sh) runs as the SSH
login user with no sudo and no Python, and reports key fingerprints and comments, never key
material, tokens, or log contents. `plan` prints commands for an operator; it runs none of them.

`preflight` is read-only too. It probes every host in parallel for what the fleet bootstrap,
glaeda-mini-enroll, local acceptance and cmux scripts/setup.sh will later require (the pinned
Xcode, its selection, licence and first launch, the Metal toolchain, tool versions on the
workload PATH, Python 3.13+ for enrollment and as the workload PATH's python3, gh on the workload
PATH, the checkout with submodules and setup artifacts, a ~/glaeda whose glaeda-mini-enroll knows the options onboarding
passes (and is at --glaeda-ref when given), power, disk, a pending macOS update, Homebrew formulas
left unpinned, secret files the login user can read, which fail on a PR runner host because PR jobs
run as that user) plus onboarding progress (candidate, node id, enrollment, runner). It
prints one host-by-check table, then each host's fixes split into what the login user can do,
what needs a password, and what needs a person. It exits 1 when any host is not ready.

`fix` runs, per host and in parallel, every repair preflight found that the login user can make
without root: a shallow cmux clone with submodules, the pinned Xcode cloned on its volume, a
relocatable CPython copied from this Mac into ~/.local, Homebrew git-fetched into an empty
/opt/homebrew the login user owns (from the --from host's /opt/homebrew over the LAN, else GitHub),
brew install of rustup, zig and python@3.13 (then brew pin, as class receipts pin toolchain strings)
with /opt/homebrew/bin/python3 linked to python3.13 for the workload PATH, and of gh (left unpinned:
no receipt names it) for cmux CI jobs that call it, Glaeda and its setup,
~/glaeda moved (clean fetch, detached checkout, refused when dirty) to --glaeda-ref or main's tip
when its glaeda-mini-enroll lacks an option onboarding passes, rustup's default and pinned
toolchains, the Metal toolchain, cmux scripts/setup.sh, and the reviewed Glaeda candidate
(downloaded once on this Mac with gh, sha256 checked, copied over). A step whose prerequisite
needs root waits for sudo-plan. Without --yes it prints what it would run; with --yes it logs each
host to its own file and ends with a fresh preflight. A rerun resumes: finished steps are skipped.

`sudo-plan` prints one script per host with every root step preflight found (xcode-select,
licence, first launch, pmset, an /opt/homebrew directory for the login user that fix then fills,
and Homebrew installs, pins and links as a Homebrew owner that is someone else). With
--run it runs each over `ssh -t`, so the operator types each host's password once. --run --ask-once
reads the password once on this Mac (the fleet shares one), checks it with `sudo -k -S true` on every
host and stops at the first refusal before any plan runs, then runs each plan as root under one
`sudo -k -S -p ''` with the password on ssh stdin, never in argv, a tty or a log. --sudoers adds
a narrow NOPASSWD rule, printed in the script for review: launchctl kickstart of the manifest's
jobs only. xcode-select and first launch stay password steps, because /Applications is writable by
the admin group and a path rule for an Xcode tool there would be passwordless root.

`share THING --from HOST` copies THING (xcode, metal, brew, rustup, candidate, python) from a
prepared host straight to each peer over the minis' LAN: `ssh -A` from this Mac to the source, which
pipes a tar to the peer's LAN address (manifest `lan`, hosts.<name>.lan_ip or its en0). The peer
stages it beside the target and moves it into place only after the copy completes and verifies; it
never replaces something already there. fix --from HOST uses the same path for the Xcode app,
Python, the candidate and the Metal toolchain. Dry run by default.

`onboard` runs the whole sequence on every host that has a node_id and a compile-lane class (or
the named ones): preflight, fix, the sudo-plans (--sudo run over ssh -t, wait for the operator,
or stop), fix again, a fresh preflight, then enrollment in parallel with a heartbeat line every 30
seconds. It ends with one table: host, class, node id, generation, step reached, result, log.
Acceptance is proved once per class and Xcode build: by default a host joins on the class's
recorded acceptance (hardware.<h>.acceptance) and enrolls without accept-local; --acceptance node
runs accept-local on the host instead. Runner registration is a hook (runner_register_command,
glaeda#1174) that nothing fills yet, so onboard ends at an eligible enrollment. Dry run by default;
never_touch hosts are refused.

`repair` is the same pipeline for hosts that are already enrolled (onboard is repair for a host
with no enrollment yet): preflight, fix, sudo-plan (printed, --sudo stop by default), then every
enrolled host that is quarantined, enrolling, not accepted or on another Glaeda than the pinned
candidate is renewed onto it with glaeda-mini-enroll --renew, re-adopting its class receipt, and
its runners are started again (a loaded runner whose listener is gone is restarted). Draining
nodes are left to their operator. Dry run by default.

`upgrade --candidate-run RUN` rolls a new candidate out. It checks the run (the dispatched
`Fleet candidate bundles` workflow, successful, from a commit on main, artifact unexpired),
downloads the macOS archive once on this Mac with gh and checks it against the build's own
receipt (and --candidate-sha256, from the run's summary, when given). Then per hardware class it
picks a seed (--seed-per-class, else the first ready host), moves ~/glaeda to main's tip, stages
the candidate, stops its runners after their current job, renews the enrollment and runs the one
accept-local, exports the class receipt, and records it with the candidate pin in the manifest
file (printing the diff). Every other host of the class then renews and adopts that receipt in
parallel; one that differs is left quarantined with the differing fields named. A rerun resumes:
a class that already records a receipt for the candidate is not seeded again, and a host already
on it is left alone. Dry run by default.

fix, share, sudo-plan --run, onboard, repair and upgrade skip a host another build holds (the fleet
host lock /Users/Shared/cmux-build-fleet/host.lock) or a glaeda-reservation/v1 marker reserves, and
exit 1; --wait waits up to 4 hours instead. Every changing step on a host checks again first.

The manifest's `candidate` pins the reviewed Glaeda candidate the fleet runs (source, sha256, run,
artifact, repo, expires). upgrade writes it; preflight, fix, onboard and repair read it.

`reserve` holds hosts for dedicated work that spans several builds (a Chromium campaign), which
the fleet host lock cannot cover between builds. It writes /Users/Shared/cmux-build-fleet/
reservation.json on each host ({"schema": "glaeda-reservation/v1", owner, purpose, since, until},
temp file then mv, mode 664), active while now < until and never longer than 72 hours ahead, so a
forgotten one expires. The owner defaults to $USER@$(hostname -s). It refuses another owner's
active reservation or an invalid marker without --force; extending your own keeps its since. Writes
and removals hold a per-host writer lock and are compare-and-swap on the marker read just before,
and each is read back. The marker is parsed by glaeda_reservation.py, as the runner hook does. `release`
removes your own or any expired marker (another owner's active one or an invalid one needs --force).
Both are dry runs without --yes. The probe reports the marker and whether a build holds host.lock
(a non-blocking shared flock, dropped at once); `check` shows an active reservation as `reserved`
(informational, like `pending`) and an expired or invalid marker as drift, and `pools` leaves
reserved members (active or invalid marker) out of `conforming`; it lists them, and lists members
whose host lock was held (`busy`) without removing them.

`borrow` reserves one idle ordinary CI member (roles ci-runner and ios-simulators only, no active
marker, no Runner.Worker, host lock free) for a developer, preferring more memory, then more cpus,
then the host name. Its owner is --owner (default $USER@$(hostname -s)) plus a random lease id, so
two sessions of one operator never share a host; release it with that owner, which the output (JSON
with --json, glaeda-developer-lease/v1) names. It re-probes after writing and releases a host that
became busy meanwhile.

The fleet compile-cache catch-up roles (cmuxterm-hq build-fleet README, "Filling the store"): the host
with role `catch-up-writer` must have the LaunchDaemon ai.manaflow.cmux-build-catch-up-fill loaded (it
runs every 15 minutes, so "not running" between ticks conforms and a non-zero last exit shows as
pending), must not run the dev-build worker (bin/worker; a drained wrapper that execs /bin/sleep conforms),
its fleet-cas node must sign (--sign-key), and it needs xcode/bin/fleet-cas-writer-build.sh. The
manifest allows one writer, never also dev-builds, and a ci-runner writer only with
overrides.runner.trustedRef. The dev-build workers (`dev-builds`) are readers: their node must be read-only
(--read-only-kv). On both, the released recipe (the target of recipes) must carry cmux-build-mode.py,
the fixed paths under xcode/ (src/cmux, DerivedData, fleet-cas.sock, fleet-cas.env,
bin/fleet-cas-marker.sh, bin/fleet-cas-settings.sh and their parents) must be real directories, a
socket and files, never symlinks (a missing src/cmux or DerivedData is pending until the first
catch-up build), and the node must answer on its socket, the connect fleet-cas-settings.sh makes,
which the probe inlines rather than running a file the fleet user can change. check only reports
these: the cache keys include xcode/src/cmux and xcode/DerivedData, so nothing here deletes or
re-clones them. sudo-plan --sudoers leaves the timer job out of its kickstart rule.

The manifest's `toolchain` (in defaults, overridable per host) declares what every build host
must carry: the Xcode app and build and whether it is selected, the Metal toolchain, the minimum
Zig, rustup's default toolchain, the minimum Python, and the cmux checkout (repo, ref, root,
submodule depth). check, preflight and fix read it from there.

`apply` only adds, and only what the manifest can prove: it appends a required key whose
`public_key` the manifest carries and whose fingerprint matches, after copying authorized_keys
aside, prefixed by the key's `options` when it declares them (from=, command=, no-pty; check then
requires exactly those options on that key, compared by a short digest the probe reports in place
of their text, which hides them from casual reading but not from guessing); and it APFS-clones an Xcode (`cp -c`, a real directory sharing blocks, never a symlink)
when the source's version and build match the target. It never removes keys, never uses sudo,
and skips hosts marked `"apply": false`. Without --yes it prints what it would run. Every run
ends with a fresh observation and check of the hosts it touched.

A real fleet manifest names hosts, people, and keys. Keep it out of this public repository:
the default path is ~/.config/glaeda/mini-fleet.json (override with --manifest or
$GLAEDA_MINI_FLEET_MANIFEST). examples/mini-fleet/manifest.example.json shows the shape.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
